When demonstrations have unusually wide/tall inputs and much smaller outputs, inspect the entire layout before assuming a cellwise transformation. Look for a separator line/column and distinct panels on either side. Across demos, compare output dimensions to the panel dimensions: if the output consistently matches one panel, the task likely extracts or transforms that panel while discarding the other, separator, or framing. Normalize colors by role and test candidate panel selection against every example.

After inferring the selected panel, determine its transformation independently from the discarded region. Do not let random-looking cells elsewhere in the input distract from a compact output panel. Apply the same spatial crop/projection to the test input, then map colors using the demos. Validate the resulting row count and each row width against the inferred output dimensions before emitting JSON. Incorrect feedback may stem from a wrong selection rule, a wrong transformation, or simply malformed output dimensions; distinguish these failure classes before spending another attempt.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md", "content": "# Noisy-background pattern propagation checkpoint

When a small colored motif is embedded in a mostly two-color noisy field and outputs add that motif color at many scattered locations, do not infer a rule from visual resemblance alone. Compare every input-output pair cell by cell and form the exact changed-cell mask. Test exact motif placements, orientations, overlaps, and near-matches; conspicuous uncolored near-matches are negative evidence that a candidate selection rule must explain. Check that original marker cells are preserved and that all additions—not merely many of them—follow one consistent criterion. Before requesting another demo, state at least two surviving rules, their distinct predictions for the active test, and the concrete example outcome that would discriminate them. A further noisy grid without such a discriminator is not an evidence-led request.

This is a general audit method, not a claim that motif-copying is the right explanation for every noisy grid. See also `noisy-region-hypothesis-audit.md` and `overlapping-pattern-copy-audit.md` for connectivity and false-positive placement pitfalls.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/failed-demo-request-loop.md", "content": "# Failed demo-request loop: enforce an evidence checkpoint

A costly ARC instance can go badly even when the assistant correctly recognizes that demos need reconciliation: repeatedly loading guidance is not the same as applying it. In this instance, six heterogeneous demos were received; the assistant made one unsupported recoloring submission, then requested additional demos without comparing exact changed-cell masks or naming live hypotheses that would be discriminated. The final received example was left unused. This is a process failure, not evidence for a particular transformation.

Reusable procedure: after every demo, pause and compare input/output changed locations, note preserved counterexamples, and test candidate rules against all pairs. Before another request, name at least two plausible rules and how a possible demo result would distinguish them and alter the test prediction. If the noisy/heterogeneous pairs do not establish a coherent rule, say so internally and stop issuing generic requests; use the best-supported prediction while budget remains. Following an incorrect submission, rebuild the full hypothesis from evidence rather than returning to requests by default. Preserve exactly one JSON action per evaluator turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/failed-demo-request-loop.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md", "content": "# Learning panel-label rules from compressed outputs

When a separator-delimited grid shrinks to one output cell per panel, the lattice and ordering are only the first inference step. A plausible grid with matching dimensions can still be wrong because the cell-label semantics are underdetermined.

## Panel evidence ledger

For every demonstration, align each panel with its output coordinate and record:
- marker/background/separator colors;
- all exceptional-cell coordinates, counts, and arrangement;
- whether features align across panel rows or columns;
- the output value for that panel.

Normalize palettes across examples. Test panel-local rules (presence, count, shape, location) and cross-panel rules (aligned feature sequences, repeated rows/columns, or spatial configurations) against every cell of every output. Distinguish selecting a panel from assigning its output label; the output can encode a relationship rather than copy the local marker color.

## Costly action checkpoint

After each demo, update the ledger before requesting another. Name the competing rules and whether they produce different predictions on the active test. After an incorrect submission, preserve only validated structural facts (such as panel dimensions/order), discard the rejected semantic mapping, and rebuild from all demonstrations. Binary feedback identifies no particular cell, so do not make arbitrary local edits. Spend another demo only when it can discriminate live rules enough to justify its fixed cost.

Always preserve the strict one-action JSON protocol when the evaluator requires it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/post-rejection-extent-color-audit.md", "content": "# Post-rejection extent and color audit

A binary wrong result rejects the complete proposed rule, not just a guessed cell. Before another request or submission, revisit all demos and partition the hypothesis into (1) trigger, (2) selected object or stroke extent, and (3) replacement color. Record exactly which cells change and which similar motifs remain untouched. Do not let a familiar visual analogy override these constraints.

For thin strokes among larger color masses, test the relation of each candidate stroke to mass boundaries and endpoints. A stroke color may be replaced by a mass color without changing the mass itself, while visually similar strokes can remain unchanged if the trigger is more specific. Verify each proposed trigger and transformed cell against every demo. A wrong first candidate is reason to rebuild the mapping, not to repeat the same theory or request another example without a concrete discriminating question.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/post-rejection-extent-color-audit.md"}
